"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"gw"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"Germany"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Wiesbaden "}}],"dateIssued":[{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"1997"},"c1997"],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"DUV, Deutscher Universitätsverlag"}},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"ger"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"German"}]},"physicalDescription":[{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"viii, 178 p. : ill. ; 21 cm."},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"illustration"}}],"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"Discusses Goldhagen's explanation of the Holocaust, accepting his thesis of personal responsibility of the perpetrators but proposing three additional factors that must be taken into account: Nazism as a pathological condition of society, the interdependence of personality and social structure, and the nature of the Nazi regime as a reign of terror. Examines Fromm's theory of the authoritarian personality, as well as a large number of studies carried out by American sociologists and psychologists during and immediately after the war (relating to Nazism in general, not to antisemitism or the Holocaust), concluding that only Fromm satisfies all four criteria. (From the Bibliography of the Vidal Sassoon International Center for the Study of Antisemitism - The Hebrew University of Jerusalem)."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Martina Parge."},"\"DUV Sozialwissenschaft\"--P. 4 of cover.","Based on the author's thesis (M.A.)--Universität Heidelberg, 1997.",{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references (p. 171-178)."}],"subject":[{"geographicCode":[{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"e-gx---"},{"@autho